%% DM26-0670

%% A push to Polar's git server, as built (2026-11-04, docs/architecture/git-server.md, cluster-bootstrap.md, "Handing off"). What the scheduler observer does next is
%% general/signed_configuration_delivery. The Cedar authorizer is designed and not built: access is the static list, by the certificate's first DNS name.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Client as A pusher (git, over mTLS)
    participant Srv as polar-git-server
    participant Git as git receive-pack
    participant Log as push-log.jsonl
    participant Cassini
    participant Sch as Scheduler observer

    Client->>Srv: git-receive-pack. The identity is the first DNS name of the client certificate
    Srv->>Srv: the repository, with write access for this identity, else 403
    Srv->>Srv: read the ref updates ahead of the pack, else 400
    Srv->>Srv: authorize each: the identity may write, under refs/heads/ only, else 403
    opt the repository names a signer (signed_by: the deployment repository)
        Srv->>Srv: read the pack into a quarantine, and verify every commit it brings<br/>to the deployment CA. A refusal is a report in git's own form, and nothing stays
    end
    Srv->>Git: only now, the push
    Git-->>Client: its report
    loop each ref that now resolves to the new commit
        Srv->>Log: time, repo, ref, old, new, pusher
        Srv-)Cassini: RepoChangedEvent on polar-git-repo-changed, if it has a broker
    end
    Cassini-)Sch: RepoChanged. Its only subscriber, and it pulls only for the repository it watches
